capability text/line-breaks  the places a line may break, and must, by UAX #14
  heroes  unicode/segment.line_breaks, unicode/segment.LineBreak
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    UAX #14 revision 55 (Unicode 17.0.0), its default rules LB1 to LB31 with LB15a to LB15d, LB19a, LB20a, LB21a and LB28a; every line of LineBreakTest.txt 17.0.0 (19,338) runs as the package's tests. Thai, Lao, Khmer and Burmese, class SA, are resolved by LB1 to AL or CM, so their runs break only at spaces and punctuation, since breaking inside their words needs a dictionary. `mandatory` is true after BK, CR, LF and NL, as ICU 78.3's rule status says, and the end of the text is mandatory only where the text ends with one. Rust's crate unicode-linebreak and ICU's line iterator are outside the three libraries

capability class/line-break  the class UAX #14 breaks lines by
  heroes  unicode/segment.line_break_class
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the value `LineBreak.txt` writes, before LB1 resolves AI, SA, SG, XX and CJ; held to ICU 78.3's Line_Break on every scalar value

capability class/east-asian-width  how wide a character is drawn in a grid of East Asian text, UAX #11
  heroes  unicode/segment.east_asian_width, unicode/segment.WIDE, unicode/segment.FULLWIDTH, unicode/segment.HALFWIDTH, unicode/segment.NARROW, unicode/segment.AMBIGUOUS, unicode/segment.NEUTRAL
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  unicodedata.east_asian_width
  note    held to Python's on every scalar value; Go's `golang.org/x/text/width` is outside the standard library

capability text/width  how many columns of a terminal a text takes
  heroes  unicode/segment.width_of, unicode/segment.width_of_east_asian
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    by grapheme, a rule of this package's, since none of the three libraries measures a text on a screen: a grapheme takes the width of its first character by UAX #11, two for an emoji drawn as one (U+FE0F after an emoji, a pair of regional indicators), one after U+FE0E, none for a control, a lone mark, a Hangul vowel or final consonant alone and a default ignorable character; the escape sequences of ECMA-48 take none. Python's `rich.cells.cell_len` and the `wcwidth` package are outside the standard library; `term` measures its tables with a table of its own until it calls this (its ledger's term/text-width)

capability text/wrap  a text wrapped to a width in columns
  heroes  unicode/segment.wrap, unicode/segment.wrap_options, unicode/segment.WrapOptions
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  textwrap.wrap, textwrap.fill, textwrap.TextWrapper, TextWrapper.wrap, TextWrapper.fill
  note    at UAX #14's break opportunities and measured by `width_of`, so ideographs break between them and a wide character counts two; Python breaks at white space and hyphens and counts characters. Its plain answers are the same for ASCII prose (observed 2026-11-17). `fill` is `wrap(...).join("\n")`. Of TextWrapper's attributes, `width`, `initial_indent`, `subsequent_indent` and `break_long_words` are WrapOptions' fields; `drop_whitespace` is always on, the spaces at a break taken away and a paragraph's leading spaces kept, as Python's default; `replace_whitespace` is off, a line break of the text ending its paragraph, where Python joins paragraphs; `break_on_hyphens` is UAX #14's, which breaks after a hyphen; `expand_tabs` and `tabsize` are `strings.expand_tabs` before wrapping; `fix_sentence_endings` is refused, its rule being, in TextWrapper's own words, "(unavoidably) imperfect"; `max_lines` and `placeholder` are the first lines of the answer and `truncate` on the last. The escape sequences of ECMA-48 are kept where they stand and take no column, which no reference library does

capability text/truncate  a text cut to a width in columns, an ellipsis after it
  heroes  unicode/segment.truncate
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  textwrap.shorten
  note    Python's `shorten` collapses white space and cuts at a word, with "[...]"; here the text is cut between graphemes with the ellipsis the program gives, every escape sequence kept, as a terminal's table cuts a cell. A program that wants whole words cuts at the last of `line_breaks` that fits

capability text/pad-columns  a text padded to a width in columns, before it, after it or on both sides
  heroes  unicode/segment.pad_start_columns, unicode/segment.pad_end_columns, unicode/segment.center_columns
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    measured by `width_of`, as `wrap` and `truncate` measure, so a column of a table in any script lines up in a terminal; Python's `str.ljust`, `str.rjust` and `str.center`, and `strings.pad_start` and its kin, count characters, so "東京" padded to 8 there takes 10 columns. A pad grapheme that would pass the width is replaced by spaces, so the answer takes exactly the width asked wherever `s` is narrower

capability segment/version  the version of Unicode the tables hold
  heroes  unicode/segment.UNICODE_VERSION
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    17.0.0, as `unicode.UNICODE_VERSION`, whose block holds Python's `unicodedata.unidata_version`; the table was made by a program from the Unicode Character Database 17.0.0 and held to Python 3.15.2's `unicodedata` and ICU 78.3 on every code point. A program using this package carries about 235 KB more than one using `unicode` alone (measured 2026-11-17, `heroes build` at -O0 on macOS)

dropped
  python  textwrap.dedent, textwrap.indent  the indentation of a text's lines, not its wrapping: `strings`' area, whose ledger names neither on 2026-11-17 (told to the round's coordinator)
